{
  "arguments": [
    {
      "claim": "The commander of Operational Group South exercised effective control over the JNA military police and, by extension, over the Territorial Defence and paramilitary forces that carried out the killings, thereby satisfying the control element of command responsibility.",
      "reasoning": "Effective control exists where a superior has the material ability to prevent or punish the commission of crimes by subordinates. The commander issued the order directing the military police to withdraw, which placed the prisoners under the custody of forces he could command. He also received regular reports from JNA officers about the conduct of those forces and had the authority to intervene, as demonstrated by his earlier attempts to improve the military police response. These factual circumstances establish that he retained the capacity to influence the actions of the perpetrators, meeting the control requirement recognized in customary international humanitarian law and the jurisprudence of the ICTY (e.g., the Čelebići case).",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commander knew, or was willfully blind to, the fact that the Territorial Defence and paramilitary forces were hostile to the prisoners and likely to commit violence against them.",
      "reasoning": "The knowledge element of command responsibility is satisfied by actual knowledge or constructive knowledge when the superior should have been aware of the risk of crimes. The commander was informed of prior killings at Velepromet, received reports of verbal abuse and threats against the prisoners at the JNA barracks, and was aware of the local Serb government's opposition to transferring the prisoners. This information created a clear expectation that the hostile forces would act violently once the military police withdrew. His failure to act on this knowledge demonstrates the requisite mens rea for command responsibility, as articulated in the customary rule on superior responsibility and affirmed by the ICTY in the case of Prosecutor v. Hadžihasanović.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The commander failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures to prevent the killings or to punish the perpetrators, thereby breaching the omission requirement of command responsibility.",
      "reasoning": "A superior must adopt and enforce effective measures to prevent or repress the commission of crimes by subordinates. The commander ordered the withdrawal of the military police without arranging alternative protection, ignored the hostile intent of the Territorial Defence, and did not intervene when reports of beatings and threats were communicated. Moreover, after the killings, he did not initiate investigations or disciplinary actions against the Territorial Defence or paramilitary participants. These omissions constitute a failure to fulfill the preventive and punitive duties imposed on commanders under customary IHL and the Rome Statute’s provisions on superior responsibility.",
      "citations": []
    },
    {
      "claim": "The mass killing of the captured Croatian combatants constitutes a grave breach of the Third Geneva Convention and therefore a war crime under the Rome Statute.",
      "reasoning": "Article 13 of the Third Geneva Convention obliges parties to treat prisoners of war humanely and prohibits willful killing. The intentional execution of over 200 prisoners who were hors de combat, after they had been surrendered and were under the protection of the ICRC, meets the definition of a grave breach. Under Article 8(2)(b)(iv) of the Rome Statute, willful killing of persons protected under the Geneva Conventions is a war crime. Although no specific treaty citation is available in the retrieved corpus, this principle is well‑established in customary international law and has been applied by the ICC in cases such as the situation in Darfur and the Prosecutor v. Al‑Bashir.",
      "citations": []
    }
  ]
}
